Standing Orders kept every build checkout and every staged release forever: 78 GB after a week here, with 306 checkouts and 121 staged releases. This PR stops that growth. (The one-off cleanup of the existing pile was done by hand, with archives on the hard-drive array.)

**Checkouts.** The worker's reconcile pass removes a clean checkout two days after it was let go. It uses `git worktree remove`, so the branch and every commit stay, and each removal is recorded in the action ledger. It never removes a checkout that:
- is leased, or held by a process (in this one or another);
- has changes, or commits that no branch, tag or remote reaches;
- belongs to an unfinished or unplaced task (failed included);
- holds a result marked complete, or a release candidate, from the last week, since a deploy installs the build from that checkout.

The keep list is read again right before each removal, and at most 20 checkouts go per pass. A lease only reuses an existing branch when it asks to (the plan lease does); every other lease still refuses one.

**Staged releases.** After a healthy deploy, older `browser-*` stages are removed, along with the database backups they hold. Kept:
- the release it installed and the one it replaced (the deployment record's, which is the way back);
- the three newest;
- any stage a launchd service or a `standing-orders` command runs from.

A stage whose deploy never finished goes once it's a week old.

**`standing-orders storage`** shows where the disk goes.

**Review.** An adversarial review ran before the gate, re-reviewed after each fix batch. It found and fixed:
- deploy-time checkouts being removed;
- detached-HEAD commits being lost;
- lease/remove races, in-process and across processes;
- unplaced tasks not being kept;
- rollback stages and CLI stages being deleted;
- silent branch reuse.

